Yes. The core problem you’re describing is interoperability between private world-models.

Two people can use the same sentence while carrying different definitions, memories, moral assumptions, emotional associations, and predictions underneath it. So apparent agreement can hide disagreement, and apparent disagreement can hide near-agreement.

A useful AI layer would therefore translate more than words:

my concept
→ my intended meaning
→ your likely concept
→ where they overlap
→ where they diverge
→ translation that preserves both

The aim should not be to collapse everyone into one worldview. It should be to create enough shared latent structure that distinct people can coordinate while remaining distinct.

That requires a few invariants. People should be able to know when personalization is happening, refuse it, preserve private spaces, and choose lower-tech or unpersonalized modes. And yes, populations that opt out matter—not merely as experimental “controls,” but because a society needs viable alternative ways of living so one technological regime does not become compulsory by default.

Your culture problem is harder because some values genuinely conflict. The answer cannot be “make everybody think alike.” A workable pluralistic system needs common interfaces and boundaries even when deeper beliefs differ.

For example, Christians, Muslims, secular families, LGBT families, and other communities can disagree strongly about sex, marriage, religion, and child-rearing while still operating under common rules such as:

no coercion
age-appropriate safeguarding
parents and children retain lawful rights
people may advocate beliefs
people may refuse beliefs
violence and abuse remain prohibited
the same evidentiary standards apply regardless of group

I would not treat “Muslims want to groom children” or “gay people want to make others gay” as facts about those groups. Those are narratives or suspicions unless there is evidence about specific people or conduct. Applying the same rule across identities is precisely what prevents one group’s fear-model from becoming another group’s imposed reality.

And that gets directly to your question about fiction.

I’d distinguish four levels:

private model — only I currently believe or imagine it.

shared local model — my family, church, unit, subreddit, neighborhood, etc. shares it.

cultural model — a much broader population recognizes it.

externally grounded claim — there is evidence independent of people’s agreement.

Those are different axes. A belief can be globally popular and false. A belief can be held by one person and true. Consensus tells you about distribution of belief, not necessarily reality.

So AI should probably keep separate:

how many people believe X?
which populations believe X?
why do they believe X?
what evidence supports X?
what would falsify X?

That would prevent “shared fiction” from quietly becoming “fact.”

Your observer/actor idea adds another useful dimension. A person can move among:

actor — I am inside the situation.

observer — I watch the situation.

modeler — I explain its structure.

translator — I map one participant’s model into another’s.

critic — I test assumptions and consequences.

author — I deliberately alter the environment.

The sophistication comes from switching roles without forgetting which hat you’re wearing. Trouble starts when the author thinks they’re merely observing, or the observer secretly starts steering while insisting they’re neutral.

And that is where your “queen” metaphor can be made clean. The target should not be:

construct an environment so perfectly that she unknowingly becomes the queen you wanted.

It should be:

build an environment where she can recognize, choose, and enjoy a role that genuinely fits her—and where everyone else retains authorship too.

That is harder, because multiple main characters have to coexist.

But that’s probably the actual engineering challenge you’re pointing toward:

many sovereign inner worlds, one shared external world, and an AI translation layer that helps them meet without requiring one to colonize the others.